description:
The City has submitted an application to the State of Florida Department of Emergency Management Hazard Mitigation Grant Program for funding available as a result of the impacts of Hurricane Milton. The Florida Division of Emergency Management is requesting to have a resolution on file that delegates signatory authority to the City Manager for this application.
The project will address mitigation for the blocks of South Daytona Avenue from 26th Street to 28th Street. The scope of work will design and construct a swale conveyance system, underground infrastructure, and stormwater retention ponds in various locations along the route. The project plan envisions the following measures (all dimensions and measurements are approximate):
1. Mill and resurface South Daytona Avenue along these two blocks (1,700 LF);
2. Install 30 or more new inlets along the east right-of-way that convey to 18" diameter HDPE pipes;
3. Install 9" deep swales along both the east and west sides of the roadway to convey water to the inlets;
4. Create three retention ponds to retain 2,800 CY of stormwater and filter the absorbed water to comply with the city's MS4 (may likely vary from this number depending on the developing plan);
5. Repair/regrade driveway aprons, add culverts for conveyance, as well as rebuilding rights-of-way to accommodate new infrastructure;
6. Regrade 20' wide utility easement between S. Daytona Avenue and John Bull Street.
7. Ultimately direct all stormwater to the ICW or the wetlands on the west side of the 2600 block of South Daytona Avenue.
Estimated FEMA Share $1,868,766
City Share $ 622,922
Total Funding $2,491,688
